WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.580
<v Narrator>So let's have a look at Amazon ECS,</v>

2
00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.230
and we're going to create our first cluster.

3
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.300
So on the left-hand side, let's click on Clusters,

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.310
and then click on Create cluster.

5
00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.050
So the cluster name can be whatever you want.

6
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.613
For example, DemoCluster.

7
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.390
And then under Infrastructure,

8
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.360
we have the way to select how to obtain capacity.

9
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.550
So now we have several options.

10
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.110
And the options is Fargate only.

11
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.330
This is serverless. You don't think about servers,

12
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.490
and AWS will provide them for you.

13
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.380
You have Fargate and Manage Instances,

14
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.840
and Fargate and Self-managed instances.

15
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.160
So this is the old way

16
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.280
in which you manage your EC2 instances,

17
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.650
and you have to create an auto-scaling group,

18
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.710
and then you have to select the type of AMI that you want,

19
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.780
the type of EC2 instance you want, and so on.

20
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.430
It's nice to see these options,

21
00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.810
but we're not going to go over this

22
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.250
because now AWS is trying to move you

23
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.650
away from self-managed instances.

24
00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:53.130
Fargate and Managed Instances is very similar

25
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.050
in which we're also going to have Fargate,

26
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.690
but also AWS is going to manage

27
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.390
our EC2 instances behind the scenes.

28
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.100
And here what we have to do

29
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.080
is to create an instance profile.

30
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.290
So you can click on Create new instance profile right here.

31
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.540
And then this is for ECS.

32
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.050
And this is for EC2 Role for ECS Managed Instances.

33
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.190
And we click on Next.

34
00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.190
And then this is called ecsInstanceRole.

35
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.330
And click on Create role.

36
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.440
So my role already exists,

37
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.630
so I'm going to just use this role right here,

38
00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:22.710
and I'm good to go.

39
00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.810
And then for infrastructure role,

40
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.240
I'm going to create a new infrastructure role.

41
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.470
I scroll down.

42
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.280
You use this one, Infrastructure for ECS Managed Instances.

43
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.900
Next. This is a new role,

44
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.090
so I'm going to create this role.

45
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.890
And now my role is created.

46
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.250
So back at it, I can just put it right here.

47
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.840
And we are good to go.

48
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.030
Next, we have the instant selection.

49
00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.670
So do we want to have the ECS default

50
00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.620
to choose based on the test definition

51
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:50.820
and service requirements?

52
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.290
And for a production workload,

53
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.910
this is definitely necessary.

54
00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.710
Or do you want to use custom?

55
00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.710
And here you would choose between the vCPU attribute

56
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.650
and the memory, say how much min and max you want.

57
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.820
So this is more advanced.

58
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.310
And also, if you wanted to force, for example,

59
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.680
a specific instance type,

60
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.570
you would say Allowed instance type.

61
00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.500
And you would say, for example, only t3.micro.

62
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.333
That's it.

63
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:15.780
And then you would only have t3.micro

64
00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.270
as part of these managed instances.

65
00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.310
So it's quite nice because AWS is going to manage

66
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.810
these instances for you.

67
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.820
And then you can go and click on Create.

68
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.160
Now, to be compatible with the next lectures that I've done,

69
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.290
but they're very similar things,

70
00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.850
instead of managing our instances,

71
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.040
we'll have self-managed instances.

72
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.830
Here we're going to create a new auto-scaling group

73
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.030
of on-demand.

74
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:40.800
We specify the instance type to be t3.micro.

75
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.880
And then we'll use the default role

76
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.650
for the EC2 instance role.

77
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.070
We say a maximum 2. No need for SSH.

78
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.780
We specify the min root EBS volume size of 30 gigabytes.

79
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.150
And then we leave everything for network settings

80
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.613
as defaults, and then click on Create.

81
00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.850
So while this is happening,

82
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.250
let's have a look at the auto-scaling group

83
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.630
that is being created on AWS.

84
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.100
And then on the left-hand side,

85
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.653
I will click on Auto Scaling Groups right here.

86
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.290
And it's showing, as you can see,

87
00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.300
there was an auto-scaling group created for me

88
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.120
called Infra-ECS-Cluster.

89
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.860
And these are capacity 0,

90
00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.260
min capacity 0, max capacity 5.

91
00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.740
And this was created directly by my ECS cluster.

92
00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.810
And the creation is in progress.

93
00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.460
And what this cluster has,

94
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.500
we'll have maybe EC2 instances

95
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.540
for me to launch tasks on.

96
00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.880
So we can see it's in three available two zones.

97
00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.170
So we know that the ECS tasks

98
00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.900
are going to be launched across three AZ.

99
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.100
And what I'm going to do now

100
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.590
is just wait for this cluster to be created,

101
00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.210
which is the case now.

102
00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.900
So what I can do now is explore this DemoCluster.

103
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.390
So if I click on it, I'm in the DemoCluster,

104
00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.670
and I can go to Services, are 0,

105
00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.340
tasks are 0 because we haven't launched anything yet.

106
00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.190
And then we go to the more interesting, Infrastructure.

107
00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.170
So if you go here to the Infrastructure,

108
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:05.100
we have three capacity providers in this ECS cluster.

109
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.120
The first one is FARGATE.

110
00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.890
That means that we can launch Fargate task

111
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.720
onto our ECS cluster.

112
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.310
The second one is FARGATE_SPOT.

113
00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.430
That means that we can launch Fargate task in Spot mode,

114
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.860
select Spot instances for EC2.

115
00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.670
And the last one is a ASG provider.

116
00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.610
And this one means that we can launch EC2 instances

117
00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.430
in this cluster directly through this ASG.

118
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.860
So it's managed scaling right now,

119
00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.080
and the current size is 0.

120
00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.300
But I can change it if I wanted to.

121
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.740
So let me show you what it would look like.

122
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.530
I go here, I go to Details,

123
00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.320
and I would edit the desired capacity to be 1,

124
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.060
just to show you what it's like.

125
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.350
So what's going to happen out of this

126
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:47.850
is that an EC2 instance is going to be created, okay?

127
00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.430
And when is created is going to register itself

128
00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.053
into the DemoCluster.

129
00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.100
And then I will see it under Container instances.

130
00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.560
That means that when we create an ECS task,

131
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.960
it can either be launched on a Fargate

132
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.300
or Fargate Spot capacity provider,

133
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.000
or it can be launched on the container instances

134
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.600
that I will have launched as part of this ASG.

135
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.470
So what I'm going to do now

136
00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:11.820
is just wait for this instance

137
00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:12.930
to be in the running state

138
00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.303
and registered into my ECS cluster.

139
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.700
So let me refresh now.

140
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.300
My instance is in service, and it's t2.micro.

141
00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.120
And if I go back into my Amazon ECS cluster,

142
00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.730
it is registered as a container instance

143
00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.830
that is currently, of course, running 0 tasks,

144
00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.370
and has 1024 CPU available,

145
00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:36.240
and 982 memory available.

146
00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.910
So this is giving me the capacity of this instance,

147
00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.310
and I can launch, as you'll see, tasks on it

148
00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.530
until the capacity runs out.

149
00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:44.730
So, we're good to go.

150
00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:45.870
So we have an ECS cluster.

151
00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:48.690
We've seen two capacity providers. Three.

152
00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.070
We've seen the container instances.

153
00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.610
So now let's go ahead and do run our first service.

154
00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.283
And I will see you in the next lecture to do that.

